Titles in food service are sometimes jokes until they are not. I ended up as **Safety Champion** — teaching and enforcing cleanliness and health standards while still floating cook, dish, and store-room coverage @cite[keys="resume-flex,wp-st-francis-story"].

This post is about the human layer: turnover, accountability, unsafe conditions, and why coworkers named me Mr. Clean, Mr. Fix-it, Superman, Aquaman, or Work Son.

## Understaffing and turnover

The dish room stayed habitually understaffed. Dishwasher turnover was high enough that building a stable crew was its own project @cite[key="wp-st-francis-story"]. Safety culture cannot be “every veteran memorizes the rules” when veterans leave every month.

Documentation and visible storage (see the reorg post) were part of making standards teachable without shaming individuals for systemic gaps.

## Reporting unsafe conditions

Part of the Operations Improvement Specialist remit was reporting unsafe working conditions — not as whistleblower theater, but as structured feedback management could act on @cite[key="ois-safety"].

That included dish-room environmental hazards tied to steam and workflow, not just “someone skipped sanitizer.”

## Weeding problem practices

Resume scratchpad language is blunt: weed out problem employees and practices @cite[key="scratchpad-st-francis"]. In practice that meant aligning with management on who could not adapt once expectations rose, and killing routines that recreated filth (see pest ecology for behavioral examples).

I did not enjoy that part. It was still cheaper than another failed inspection.

## Cleaning Responsibilities Philosophy

The indexed Cleaning Responsibilities Philosophy doc frames how duties should split across roles @cite[key="sop-cleaning-philosophy"]. Safety Champion work was the live enforcement layer on top of that philosophy — showing new hires what “done” looks like.

## Nicknames as feedback

Nicknames were coworker humor, but they tracked a real pattern: I was the person who fixed the scrubber, rewrote dish flow, and scrubbed floors on hands and knees. **Work Son** was the teasing version of “he does the maintenance department’s homework.”

